{
  "header": {
    "_comment": "EXPECTED VALUES DERIVED FROM CAPTURED LIVE BYTES. The bytes in captured-2026-08-14-sensex-live.json are ground truth from the venue; the values here are ONE ORACLE'S READING of those bytes. This file does NOT prove the oracle is correct - it establishes what Zerodha's own official Python client reads from packets Zerodha actually sent. That is a strictly stronger claim than fixtures.json, whose frames were CONSTRUCTED from the published layout and could share a misreading with any decoder tested against them.",
    "oracle": "kiteconnect 5.2.1 (_split_packets + _parse_binary)",
    "derived_by": "AT Architecture Discussions pane - NOT the author of the Rust decoder",
    "derived_from": "crates/adapters/zerodha/test_data/captured-2026-08-14-sensex-live.json",
    "source_records_sha256": "a162970d6c0c6806d451b6c65d20f7fcc4378f2dc292179ebda913b547f263b7",
    "note": "A record is a WS MESSAGE; packets[] are the individual packets inside it.",
    "packet_total": 20,
    "pairing_verification": "packets[i] <-> expected[i] is VERIFIED, not assumed: for all 20 pairs the instrument token read from packet bytes[0:4] as big-endian u32 equals the oracle's instrument_token. 20/20 matched.",
    "layout_coverage": {
      "8": "ltp/tradable",
      "28": "quote/index",
      "32": "full/index",
      "44": "quote/tradable",
      "184": "full/tradable (depth 5x5)"
    },
    "what_this_does_not_establish": "Fidelity of the ORACLE. kiteconnect could misread a field and this file would encode the same misreading. What it removes is the weaker circle: the FRAMES are no longer ours.",
    "timestamp_encoding": "exchange_timestamp and last_trade_time are INTEGER EPOCH SECONDS, exactly as they sit in the packet as big-endian u32. They were previously rendered strings, which was a defect: kiteconnect's datetime.fromtimestamp() takes no tzinfo and returns a NAIVE datetime in the HOST's local zone, so the same oracle on the same bytes emits a different string on an IST host than on a UTC one - and the file did not record which. Caught by RustClientBuilder.",
    "timestamp_verification": "All 15 of 15 timestamp values were LOCATED IN THE PACKET BYTES as big-endian u32 before conversion, so the values are read from the venue's bytes and no timezone assumption is encoded anywhere. CAVEAT: where exchange_timestamp and last_trade_time hold the SAME value (a trade in the same second), the byte-offset scan cannot distinguish which offset belongs to which field - it confirms the VALUES, not the field-to-offset mapping."
  },
